2026-11-19  Saddam  <saddamr3e@gmail.com>

	dxf_read_file: keep page-aligned DXF input NUL-terminated
	Since eada5b4 a DXF that needs no trailing newline (ASCII ending in \n,
	or any binary DXF) is mapped without overshoot and without a NUL, which
	then only exists in the zero-filled tail of a partial last page.  When
	the file size is a multiple of the page size the readers run off the
	mapping: strlen/strcpy in the binary branch of dxf_read_string, sscanf
	for a \M+ MIF char (it strlen()s its input), and strtol/strtod, which
	skip newlines.

	Map such input only when it ends with a NUL or its size is not a
	multiple of 4K, else fall back to dat_read_size, which terminates it.

	No testcase: the overread goes past an mmap, which ASan does not track,
	and it only faults when the following page is unmapped.

	* src/dwg.c (dxf_read_file): Do not mmap a page-aligned DXF that does
	  not end with a NUL.

2026-11-19  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	doc: add a Security Advisory chapter
	Warn that the C library likely still has memory corruption and
	out-of-bounds bugs, with a huge attack vector (150 MB), though it is
	constantly fuzzed and AI-checked.  Not recommended with untrusted input
	in non-hardened environments such as WASM converters on the open web, or
	for accepting JSON.  Better hardened than AutoCAD(R) though.

