2026-11-04  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	in_dxf: never file ATTRIB/VERTEX/SEQEND under a block header
	DWGs written by dxf2dwg stored ATTRIB/VERTEX/SEQEND unlike AutoCAD
	does: ODA-based readers silently dropped the ATTRIBs of every INSERT
	and the last VERTEX of every POLYLINE (a 3-vertex 3D polyline came
	back with 2). The importer filed them under the block header (their
	DXF 330 points at the block record, how ezdxf and AutoCAD write DXF),
	with entmode 2, while the SEQEND (owner rewritten to the parent by
	dxf_postprocess_SEQEND) silently failed add_to_BLOCK_HEADER and stayed
	entmode 0, outside every chain.

	Two fixes:

	* dxf_entities_read never files ATTRIB/VERTEX*/SEQEND under a block
	  header: they belong to their parent INSERT/POLYLINE, not the block.

	* dxf_postprocess_SEQEND sets each child's ownerhandle to the parent
	  entity and entmode 0, whatever their 330 pointed to - same for the
	  SEQEND itself.

	After this the emitted structure matches the ODA reference field for
	field: subentity entmode 0, ownerhandle the parent, block header chain
	top-level entities only. ODA reads back all vertices and attributes.

	Reported-by: tuxiasumari

2026-11-04  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	dwg2SVG: fix blank output (viewBox origin, stroke width, model space)
	example_r14.dwg rendered blank for four independent reasons; each one
	alone is enough:

	1. Model space was never drawn. output_BLOCK_HEADER returns how many
	   entities we did print, and output_SVG falls back to model space when
	   that is zero. But output_object started at num=1 and only the
	   default branch cleared it, so it returned 1 for every type it
	   recognised, including VIEWPORT and SEQEND which draw nothing, and
	   for entities skipped as invisible. Every paper space holds a
	   VIEWPORT, so the count was never zero and the fallback never fired.
	   The walk now counts only entities that actually put coordinates on
	   the page.

	2. Every stroke was zero width. dxf_cvt_lweight gives 100ths of a mm,
	   so mm is lw/100, not lw/1000. With the extra factor of ten a 0.25mm
	   line became 0.025, which %.1f printed as stroke-width:0.0. And
	   lw==0 is a legitimate value meaning thinnest line. Widths are now
	   emitted in mm with vector-effect:non-scaling-stroke, which keeps a
	   hairline visible whatever the viewBox spans.

	3. The viewBox origin contradicted the coordinates. transform_X
	   returns x-model_xmin and transform_Y returns
	   page_height-(y-model_ymin), so every emitted coordinate lives in
	   [0,page_width]x[0,page_height]. The viewBox started at
	   (model_xmin, model_ymin) instead of (0,0), displacing the content
	   out of view by exactly the model origin.

	On 95 real drawings from a 1657-file corpus, each rendered to a
	200x200 PNG and scored by ink pixels: drawings that show a drawing
	went from 1 to 68; 0 got worse; total ink pixels 80 -> 55775.

	Fixes #523 and #1012 (partially - the defs-symbols double-translation
	and the viewBox being the header box rather than the drawing box are
	follow-ups).

	Reported-by: tuxiasumari

2026-11-04  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	decode: treat a missing pre-R13 table sentinel as a warning
	An r11 drawing here has three empty tables (VIEW, UCS, VPORT) that the
	writer only terminated with a single VPORT_END sentinel, occupying the
	16 bytes where APPID_BEGIN would go. The decoder rejected the whole file
	(4060 errors, no DXF output) over the missing delimiters, although all
	553 entities and the LAYER/STYLE/LTYPE tables had already decoded fine.

	The sentinel is a delimiter, not the locator: records are read from
	tbl->address (stated independently by the header) and each carries its
	own CRC, which is already verified. A wrong address is caught on its own
	merits instead of being inferred from a missing delimiter.

	So a sentinel that is genuinely absent now logs a warning and reads the
	table from its header address, carrying on. DWG_ERR_INVALIDDWG stays
	fatal: that one means the 16 bytes could not be read at all, so the
	position itself is nonsense.

	The not-found message becomes a warning for the same reason: fatality is
	the caller's decision, and a file that now converts perfectly still
	logged six ERROR lines, which any CI or bench grepping for ERROR reads
	as a failure.

	Fixes #767.

	Reported-by: tuxiasumari

2026-11-04  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	decode: resync the object map after a failed modular char read
	bit_read_UMC returns 0 on nine or more consecutive high-bit bytes, and the
	handle map loop then warned and used the garbage anyway, fabricating a
	multi-megabyte object and derailing every entry after it.

	Resync one byte later instead of trusting the failed read, so the stray
	bytes are skipped and the entry stream resumes on the page boundary.
	Only on !handleoff: the other conditions in the same check also fire on
	entries that parsed fine, and resyncing on them costs good entries
	(Leader.dwg would lose all of its modelspace entities).

	Fixes #1355.

	Reported-by: tuxiasumari

2026-11-04  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	dwg: skip unresolvable owned entities instead of ending the layout
	get_first_owned_entity, get_next_owned_entity and
	get_next_owned_block_entity return NULL both for 'the list is over' and
	for 'this entry does not resolve', and the walkers (out_dxf, out_dxfb,
	dwggrep, dwg2SVG) use while (obj). So one dangling reference ended the
	whole layout and every entity behind it was dropped, silently, with exit
	status 0.

	Skip the unresolvable entries (logging a warning for each) instead, and
	use < rather than == against num_owned: with ==, an iterator bumped past
	the count never terminates and walks off the end of entities[].

	Reported-by: tuxiasumari

