2026-11-04  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	decode: accept valid EED of exactly 256 bytes (BS code 10)
	dwg_decode_eed rejected extended data whose size was exactly 256 bytes:
	a BS value of 256 is the 2-bit code 10 with no payload, so reading it
	moves the bit position by only 2 bits and dat->byte can stay equal to
	sav_byte (the byte where the EED started). The "no progress" check
	dat->byte == sav_byte then treated the valid data as a stall, and the
	rest of the entity decoded from the wrong position (LINE vertices came
	out like 1e236).

	Limit that byte check to the pre-R13 RS size, which always reads two
	bytes; the BS path (R13+) can legitimately not advance the byte.

	* src/decode.c (dwg_decode_eed): only apply the dat->byte == sav_byte
	  check for from_version < R_13b1.

	Fixes GH #1492.
	Reported-by: hjkim-fieldro

2026-11-03  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	out_dxf: emit an empty chunk for a NULL binary payload
	VALUE_BINARY wrote `size` 00 bytes whenever the payload pointer was NULL
	but the declared length was nonzero. A crafted OLEFRAME with data ==
	NULL and a multi-gigabyte data_size made dwg2dxf spin a core emitting
	zeros. The decode-side guard (GHSA-37mv) only covers the binary-DWG read
	path; an API/JSON/DXF-built dwg still reaches this sink on DXF export.

	Emit an empty chunk for a NULL payload instead of that many zeros. The
	DXFB and JSON writers already guard on the pointer, so this closes the
	DXF path of the binary-size validation class.

	Harden GHSA-fxmw-mp89-7g88 (output-writer side).

	* src/out_dxf.c (VALUE_BINARY): emit an empty chunk for a NULL payload
	  with a nonzero declared length, instead of writing `size` zeros.

