2026-11-03  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	POINTCLOUD: set clippings num_vertices to 2 only when decoding
	dwg2.spec POINTCLOUD had the same shape for its clippings as IMAGE and
	WIPEOUT for their clip boundary: num_vertices = 2 for every clipping
	but a polygon (type 3), in every action. But only the DWG has no count
	for a box or a rectangle. The JSON importer allocates vertices at the
	length of the JSON array, and takes num_vertices from it, as it does
	for every num_ field. So a JSON clipping

	  { "type": 2, "vertices": [[-0.5, -0.5]] }

	makes the r2013+ encoder read vertices[1] past the 1-element array:

	  $ dwgwrite -y --as r2018 pc.json
	  ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow ... READ of size 8
	      #0 in dwg_encode_POINTCLOUD_private src/dwg2.spec
	  allocated by: xcalloc <- _set_struct_field src/in_json.c

	This does for POINTCLOUD what IMAGE and WIPEOUT do: only the DECODER
	sets the count to 2. The decoder reads exactly 2 verts for a box or a
	rectangle, so the ENCODER, the DXF writer and the JSON writer write a
	non-polygon clipping with any other count as a polygon, with its
	count, and warn. The written file then reads back as imported.

	The rectangles and boxes of a DWG still decode to 2 verts, so the
	DXF, DXFB and JSON output of DWG files, and their re-encoding, do not
	change.

	While testing it, dwgread -O JSON and then dwgwrite failed on every
	DWG with POINTCLOUD clippings: out_json has no SUB_FIELD_2RD_VECTOR or
	SUB_FIELD_3BD_VECTOR, so the default from spec.h writes each point
	under its own "vertices[vcount]" key ("pts[vcount]" for POINTCLOUDEX
	croppings). in_json does not know that key and loses its place, and
	dwgwrite stops with "Unexpected JSON key" without writing a DWG. This
	writes them as arrays, as FIELD_2RD_VECTOR and FIELD_3DPOINT_VECTOR
	do, and frees them in free.c, which had neither macro.

	The new test test_pointcloud_clippings reads the PoC POINTCLOUD, a
	rect clipping with a single vertex, and checks that num_vertices and
	the vertices follow the array.

	See GH #1487.

	Reported-by: Mar <mardgez13@gmail.com>

