2026-11-03  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	decode: reject overlarge OLEFRAME/OLE2FRAME data_size on import
	GHSA-37mv-q3xr-pjjc: DoS via a crafted DWG carrying an OLEFRAME/OLE2FRAME
	whose data_size is ~3.85 GB (3851730860) while data is NULL. dwg2dxf then
	emits gigabytes of zeros in its VALUE_BINARY loop. Catch the bogus length
	at import/decode time so it never reaches the writer.

	The OLE payload (the 310 data field) is stored inline in the object, so
	data_size can never exceed the object/document size. The R2013+ object
	section is decompressed in-place into the object stream (no separate OLE
	binary string stream), so bounding data_size against dat->size is safe for
	all versions and cannot false-positive. The check runs before the binary
	read so a bogus length cannot drive the bit_read_TF allocation, and before
	the xcalloc in in_dxf.c.

	src/dwg.spec: add a DECODER guard to OLEFRAME and OLE2FRAME that logs and
	  zeroes data_size when it exceeds dat->size, placed before FIELD_BINARY.
	src/in_dxf.c: in the OLE2FRAME 90 handler, reject (goto invalid_dxf) when
	  the pair value exceeds dat->size, before the xcalloc of the data buffer.
	  Cast pair->value.l to size_t/BITCODE_BL to keep -Werror=sign-compare and
	  -Werror=format clean on 32-bit and strict builds.
