	decode: size each EED item's data to the item, not the rest of the EED
	dwg_decode_eed allocated every EED item with calloc (size + 8, 1),
	where size was what is left of the whole EED block, not the size of
	the item. Since the decoder keeps splitting the remainder into new
	items, an object whose EED holds many small items took memory
	quadratic in the number of items: a 20,000-item XDATA line needed
	556 MB of peak RSS in dwg2dxf, and large P&ID drawings with proxy
	EED ran out of memory.

	After an item is decoded, realloc its data down to the bytes it
	consumed plus 8 (the string header/terminator slack the decoder
	stores beyond the read bytes), never below sizeof (Dwg_Eed_Data)
	for the fixed union members.

	Measured peak RSS of dwg2dxf on the 20,000-item XDATA line:
	556 MB -> 51 MB. DXF output is byte-identical for blank_Acad2004,
	2000/2, example_2007, example_2013 and example_2018.

	Reported by tuxiasumari, https://github.com/tuxiasumari (PR #1426)

2026-10-30  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	out_dxf: export dxf_block_is_layout so the SWIG module links
	dxf_block_is_layout is declared in dwg.h, so the SWIG Python wrapper
	generates a reference to it, but it was neither marked EXPORT nor defined
	in an always-compiled TU: on Windows EXPORT is __declspec(dllexport), so an
	unmarked symbol is absent from the DLL export table and the link of the
	SWIG module fails with "undefined reference to dxf_block_is_layout".

	Mark it EXPORT (like the sibling dxf_cvt_lweight) and move its definition
	from out_dxf.c (only built when !DISABLE_DXF) next to dxf_cvt_lweight in
	dwg.c, which is always compiled. The body is unchanged.

	* include/dwg.h: mark dxf_block_is_layout() EXPORT.
	* src/dwg.c: define dxf_block_is_layout() after dxf_revcvt_lweight().
	* src/out_dxf.c: drop the definition.

2026-10-30  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>
	    omp + qwen3.8-27b-fp8-s2  <noreply@qwen.ai>

	out_dxf: number layout viewports per block for DXF groups 68/69
	DXF groups 68 (stacking order) and 69 (viewport id) are not stored
	in the DWG; the writer derives them, and derived them from entmode
	alone. entmode 0 does not mean "the overall paperspace viewport", it
	means the entity carries an explicit owner handle -- which is how
	every layout stores its entities except the one current when the
	file was saved (entmode 1). So in a drawing with more than one
	layout, the viewports of every other layout were written off
	(68 = 0, 69 = 0), and a consumer that honors the status draws an
	empty sheet.

	Derive the owning block the same way as group 330 in
	common_entity_handle_data.spec (the explicit owner handle, else the
	paperspace/modelspace block record the entmode implies) and number
	the viewport 1..N within that block, restarting when the block
	changes. 68 gets the same number as 69, as it is the stacking order
	("1 is the active viewport, 2 the next"), not a boolean. Viewports
