	timebll: compute the julian day fraction from milliseconds
	A TIMEBLL/TIMERLL is a julian day number plus the milliseconds elapsed
	in that day, so the fraction of a day is ms / 86400000. The tree
	computed it three mutually incompatible ways: days + ms * 1e-8 in
	bits.c, header_variables.spec, header_variables_r11.spec and
	dwg_api.c (off by a factor of 0.864, up to 3.3 hours), 0.864 * ms /
	1000 in cvt_TIMEBLL, and days + 86400 * ms in json_TIMEBLL (off by
	twelve orders of magnitude one way and 1000x the other).

	dwg_add_Document fills the field itself with 1000 * (now % 86400) --
	milliseconds -- and on real drawings the ms value only makes sense as
	milliseconds of the day, settling the intended reading.

	* src/bits.c
	  - bit_read_TIMEBLL, bit_read_TIMERLL: value = days + ms / 86400000.0
	* src/common.c
	  - cvt_TIMEBLL: the seconds of the day are ms / 1000, not
	    0.864 * ms / 1000 (a $TDUCREATE of [2454831, 77383890] is now
	    21:29:43 instead of 18:34:19)
	* src/header_variables.spec
	  - the TDCREATE/TDUPDATE timezone-adjusted values use ms / 86400000.0
	* src/header_variables_r11.spec
	  - the TDUCREATE value uses ms / 86400000.0; the TDUUPDATE value now
	    uses its own days/ms instead of TDUPDATE's (copy-paste slip)
	* src/dwg_api.c
	  - dwg_add_Document: TDUCREATE.value uses ms / 86400000.0
	* src/in_json.c
	  - json_TIMEBLL: the [days, ms] form and the scalar form both use
	    86400000.0 as the number of milliseconds of a day

	The value field is display-only: bit_write_TIMEBLL/TIMERLL ignore it
	and write days/ms, so no DWG bytes change. Verified against ODA
	FileConverter, which agrees to every printed digit ($TDCREATE
	2451380.847099063, $TDUCREATE 2451380.805432396 for a test drawing);
	corpus DWGs now differ from the previous output only in the six $TD
	display lines, and the JSON [days, ms] and scalar roundtrips import
	unchanged.

	Reported by tuxiasumari, https://github.com/tuxiasumari (PR #1369)

