2026-10-12  Jamie Jahn  <jamiejahnio@gmail.com>

	MESH: DXF 91 is the subdivision level, not a vertex count
	dwg2.spec read 91 as the size of a subdiv_vertex array and consumed
	level * 3BD before num_vertex. Every MESH with a smoothness level
	above 0 was misaligned from there on, so vertex, faces, edges and
	crease decoded as garbage. Only the level 0 mesh is stored.

	in_dxf no longer allocates subdiv_vertex from group 91. The field
	keeps its name, so the API does not change; it now holds the level.

2026-10-12  Aleksei Korolev  <lllshamanlll@gmail.com>

	programs: initialise optind once, not on every getopt call
	The bundled getopt, compiled where the system has no <getopt.h> — in
	practice MSVC — assigned opterr and optind at the top of getopt() itself.
	optind is the scan position, so every call reset it to 1 and

	    while ((c = getopt (argc, argv, "...")) != -1)

	never terminated: the first option was parsed and optind advanced, then
	the next call rewound and parsed the same option again, forever. Any
	option at all made the programs spin on one core; "dxf2dwg -a r2000" with
	no input file is enough to reproduce it. An invocation with no options
	works, which is what made this look intermittent: argv[1] then does not
	begin with '-', so the first call returns -1 through the one branch that
	never reaches the option handling.

	The original BSD getopt initialises these at file scope. That was lost
	when the declarations moved into my_getopt.h, where one GETOPT_EXTERN
	macro cannot carry the initialisers — written that way they would define
	the variables in every translation unit that includes the header. So the
	macro gives way to two explicit branches: definitions with their initial
	values under GETOPT_C, plain extern declarations elsewhere.

	Initialising in getopt.c next to the existing tentative definitions is a
	smaller diff, but it leaves the header still unable to express an initial
	value, which is the trap that produced this bug, and relies on tentative
	definitions that C++ does not have.

	Checked with MSVC 19.44 on Windows: before, "dxf2dwg -o out.dwg in.dxf"
	consumed a full core until killed; after, it writes a DWG byte-identical
	to the one a GNU/Linux build produces from the same drawing.

	Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

