2026-10-01  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	in_dxf: keep each application's EED apart when an object has several
	An object whose DXF carries XDATA from two or more applications came
	out of dxf2dwg with every group after the first fused into the first:
	ODA, AutoCAD and dwg2dxf all read one application holding everybody's
	values. Two faults in add_eed, both needed for the fix:

	* A 1001 fills slot i with its APPID handle and gives the slot back
	  (num_eed--) so that the group's first value reuses it. On reuse the
	  slot is realloc'd and memset to 0 whenever i > 0, which wipes the
	  handle the 1001 just stored. Only the first group (i == 0) escaped.
	  The memset is meant for a freshly grown slot; skip it for the one
	  the 1001 reserved.

	* "search for previous size index" walks down from i without stopping,
	  so it always settles on the lowest slot with a handle -- the first
	  group -- and adds every later value's size to it. Stop at the
	  nearest one, which is what the comment describes.

	Reproducer (a LINE with two applications):

	  0 ... LINE ... 1001 APPA 1000 first 1001 APPB 1000 second 1070 1
	  dxf2dwg -y --as r2000 -o two.dwg two.dxf
	  dwg2dxf -y -o back.dxf two.dwg   # APPA: first, second, 1  (APPB gone)

	Measured on 42 real drawings (DXF -> dxf2dwg r2000 -> dwg2dxf): of the
	851 objects with two or more EED groups, 0 came back intact before and
	851 after; the 19658 single-group objects are unchanged. ODA reads the
	written DWG the same as it reads the source DXF. make check passes.

	Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-10-01  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	in_dxf: resolve the ACAD EED application through the APPID table
	add_eed hard-codes 5.1.12 as the handle of the ACAD application. That
	is where AutoCAD puts it, but not every DXF does: ezdxf, for one,
	creates it at 2A. The written EED then points at whatever object sits
	at 0x12, ODA and AutoCAD cannot resolve the application, audit it into
	a "$TD_AUDIT_GENERATED_(12)" group, and the data it carries is lost --
	for a DIMENSION that is its DSTYLE overrides.

	Look ACAD up in the APPID table like every other application, and keep
	5.1.12 only as the fallback when the table has no ACAD entry.

	Reproducer: a linear dimension with a dimlfac override written by
	ezdxf (APPID ACAD at 2A), dxf2dwg --as r2000, then read with ODA:
	before, the dimension has no dimlfac override; after, dimlfac = 0.05.
	make check passes.

	Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-09-10  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	tests: fix ODA hangs
	redirect stdin from /dev/null for ODAFileConverter calls

	timeout(1) puts the monitored command into a new process group but
	never makes it the controlling terminal's foreground group. Under
	make check (running attached to a real tty/pty), xvfb-run's own
	`stty size` probe (used to size its diagnostic messages) then reads
	the controlling terminal from that background-relative group, and
	the kernel delivers a job-control stop (SIGTTIN) to the whole group.
	Nothing ever resumes it, so `timeout N xvfb-run ODAFileConverter ...`
	hangs forever instead of being bounded by the timeout.

	Fix by redirecting stdin from /dev/null on every
	`timeout ... $ODAFileConverter ...` invocation, so no component in
	that subtree can touch the controlling terminal in the first place.

	* dxf-check: add </dev/null to both ODA roundtrip calls.
	* dxfb-check: add </dev/null to both ODA roundtrip calls.
	* json-check: add </dev/null to the ODA roundtrip call.
	* oda: add </dev/null to the ODA roundtrip call.
	* programs/dxf.test.in: add </dev/null to both ODA calls
	  (check_subentity, check_roundtrip).
	* programs/dxfb.test.in: add </dev/null to the ODA call.
	* examples/dwgadd_test.sh.in: add </dev/null to the checkODA call.

	Reproduced and verified fixed under a real pty (make check and
	standalone runs); confirmed hanging without the fix and completing
	in <1s with it, with no orphaned Xvfb/ODAFileConverter processes
	left behind.

2026-09-10  Hallel  <hallel.kula@gmail.com>

	dxf: emit the group for a value that quotes away to nothing
	dxf_write_chunked() is a bare while (len > 0), and dxf_fixup_string()
	emits the empty-value form only from its outer else branch.  A non-empty
	input whose quoted form came out empty therefore emits no group code at
	all, and every following tag in the file shifts by one.

	This is hardening, not a second live defect, and I would rather say so
	than overstate it: I could not construct an input that reaches it on
	master.  cquote()'s invalid-MIF path needs s + 6 < send to enter and
	consumes only 4 bytes, so it always leaves trailing bytes for the else
	branch to write, and bit_embed_TU() does not return an empty string for
	a non-empty input.  What it closes is the same "the length is whatever
	strlen says" assumption as the previous commit.

	Output is unchanged on all 184 drawings tested there, as expected for a
	guard on an unreachable state.

	PR #1412

		* src/out_dxf.c (dxf_write_chunked): Write an empty value for a
		zero-length string rather than nothing at all.

2026-09-10  Hallel  <hallel.kula@gmail.com>

	dxf: terminate cquote's output at the last byte written
	cquote() stores its NUL at d[len - 1], the last byte of the destination
	buffer, and never at the position where it stopped writing.
	dxf_fixup_string() then calls strlen() on that buffer, so every byte
	between the end of the quoted string and the end of the allocation is
	read back as string content.  The buffer is malloc'd with
	(2 * emblen) + 1 bytes, so any string that grows by less than 2x under
	quoting leaves such a gap.

	Reproduces with the shipped test data, no allocator tricks needed:

	  programs/dwg2dxf --as r2018 -y -o out.dxf test/test-data/example_2018.dwg
	  grep -a Fgdt out.dxf

	  expected   {\Fgdt;r}%%v1%%v2%%v%%v%%v^J10
	  actual     {\Fgdt;r}%%v1%%v2%%v%%v%%v^J10v

	The trailing byte is whatever the allocation happened to hold: the value
	quotes to 30 bytes in a 59-byte buffer, and strlen() runs past the end of
	what was written.  Same on example_2004, _2007, _2010 and _2013.  Under
	MALLOC_PERTURB_ the residue grows to a long run rather than a single
	byte, which is how it was first spotted.

	This is the defect valgrind reported against MTEXT in 3c5ab3bb.  That
	commit moved the buffer to a zeroed static, which changed the residue
	from arbitrary heap to the previous call's tail rather than fixing the
	termination; the buffer is malloc'd again today.

	PR #1412

		* src/out_dxf.c (cquote): Terminate after the last byte written,
		clamped to the end of the buffer.

