2026-08-10  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	decode: widen the pre-R13 sentinel search to the +-1000 it documents
	A r11 drawing here was rejected outright:

	    ERROR: DWG_SENTINEL_R11_LAYER_BEGIN not found at 67586
	    ERROR: Failed to decode file ... 0x101
	    READ ERROR 0x101

	The file is fine. Every 16-byte table sentinel in it sits earlier than where the
	header's table address puts it:

	    BLOCK_BEGIN   expected 67286, actually 67254   (-32)
	    LAYER_BEGIN   expected 67586, actually 67286   (-300)
	    STYLE_BEGIN   expected 67741, actually 67586   (-155)
	    VIEW_BEGIN    expected 67971, actually 67363   (-608)

	decode_preR13_sentinel already recovers from exactly this: when the sentinel is
	not where expected it searches around, repositions, and continues with a
	WRONGCRC warning. But the window was

	    pos = MAX (dat->byte, 200) - 200;
	    len = MIN (dat->size - dat->byte, 400);
	    // search +- 1000 bytes around

	+-200, while its own comment promised +-1000. LAYER_BEGIN at -300 fell outside
	by 116 bytes, so the recovery never fired and the file was lost. Widened to the
	documented window, and computing len from pos so it really covers both
	directions.

	Two r11 drawings that produced no output at all now decode to exactly what ODA
	File Converter reads from them:

	    primer-piso.dwg    nothing -> 1246 modelspace entities  (ODA: 1246)
	    segundo-piso.dwg   nothing -> 1459                      (ODA: 1459)

	make check: 270 PASS / 0 FAIL, unchanged. All 146 DWGs in test/test-data and
	programs/ re-converted before and after: 146 identical, 0 worse — upstream's own
	r11 files have their sentinels where the header says, so nothing there changes.
	A 190-drawing corpus sweep: NO_OUTPUT 3 -> 1, OK 167 -> 169, zero regressions.

2026-08-10  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	dwg_read_dxf: a DXF without a trailing newline lost its last group pair
	    // properly end the buffer for strtol()/... readers
	    if (dat.chain[size - 1] != '\n')
	      {
	        dat.chain[size] = '\n';
	        dat.size++;
	      }
	    dat.chain[size] = '\0';

	The newline is appended at chain[size] and then overwritten by the NUL at
	the same index. dat.size was incremented, so the buffer ends "...\n0\0"
	with size pointing past a NUL that stands where the newline should be.

	dxf_read_string then refuses to read the value:

	    if (dat->byte >= dat->size
	        || !memchr (&dat->chain[dat->byte], '\n', dat->size - dat->byte))
	      return;

	It leaves *string NULL, the caller reports that as "Out of memory" -- on
	an 18 KB file -- and the whole import fails with DWG_ERR_IOERROR. The
	chain is calloc'd with two spare bytes for exactly this case, so the fix
	is to put the NUL after the newline.

	The same DXF, differing only in whether the last byte is a newline:

	    with a trailing newline      5139-byte DWG
	    without                      failed, no output

	and with this patch both produce the same 5139 bytes, byte for byte.

	That is the whole class: any DXF whose last byte is not a newline is
	rejected, whatever it contains, because the loss falls on the final
	0/EOF. The file attached to #474 (from Maker.js, ending "0\nEOF") is one,
	and it now converts to a 12696-byte AC1015 DWG instead of failing.

	#474 is not closed by this. The DWG it now produces is still rejected by
	ODA File Converter with "Null object Id: <object> (0)", which is the
	diagnosis rurban gave in that thread in 2022: a minimal DXF carries no
	handles, and handles are used internally to find table entries, so they
	have to be assigned. That is a separate fix. This one only stops the
	import failing outright, and fixes every other DXF that happens to end
	without a newline.

	Verification:

	- make check: 270 PASS / 0 FAIL / 0 SKIP, unchanged.
	- Eight real DXFs (26 KB to 47 MB) through dxf2dwg before and after:
	  every DWG byte-identical. Two of them produce no output on either side
	  (unrelated), the other six match exactly.
	- Built and tested on a clean e405fcff (= tag 0.14.8556) with this patch
	  alone. Ubuntu 26.04, gcc 15.2.0.

2026-08-10  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	encode: in_postprocess_SEQEND rewrapped a relative handle value as absolute
	The first/last_attrib (and first/last_vertex) refs collected by
	dxf_postprocess_SEQEND are created with an owner, so dwg_add_handle may
	store their handleref.value as a relative offset. Rewrapping that value
	with dwg_add_handleref (dwg, 4, owned[0]->handleref.value, NULL) then
	produces a dangling absolute handle: an INSERT with one ATTRIB (0x53,
	owner 0x51) got first_attrib = 2, pointing at the LTYPE control object.

	ODA-based readers reject the whole drawing over it:
	  Object improperly read: <AcDbBlockReference>
	  Previous error: Object of class AcDbLinetypeTable can't be cast to AcDbEntity
	i.e. every DWG written by dxf2dwg from a DXF with block attributes was
	unreadable in AutoCAD. Use absolute_ref, as dwg_dup_handleref does.

2026-08-10  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	in_dxf: a SPLINE with only fit points is scenario 2, not an empty scenario 1
	The scenario was decided solely from flag bit 32, which is LibreDWG's
	own marker, not a DXF one: standard group 70 has no fit-vs-control bit.
	A SPLINE carrying only fit points (72=0, 73=0, 74=n — what ezdxf's
	add_spline writes) stayed scenario 1 and encoded zero knots and zero
	control points: an empty spline, silently gone after dxf2dwg. The
	correct assignment was already there at the 74 handler, commented out;
	guard it so splines that do carry knots/control points keep scenario 1.

	bits: keep the palette index when downconverting a CMC whose rgb round-trips
	bit_downconvert_CMC rederived the index from the rgb even when a valid
	palette index was already there. Several ACI entries share one rgb
	(1/10 are both FF0000, 5/170 both 0000FF, ...) and dwg_find_color_index
	returns the first match, so writing r2000 from an r2004+ source silently
	recolored entities: color 170 became 5, color 10 became 1.

	in_dxf: group 420 lost its method and rgb-present flag — true color dropped
	The common-entity 420 handler set color.method = value >> 24, which is 0
	for any plain RGB, an invalid CMC method the encoder then discards. And
	the r2004+ entity color encoding writes the rgb only under the 0x80
	rgb-present flag, which nothing set (the 440 handler right below does
	set its 0x20). Every true color imported from DXF was dropped when
	writing r2004. Sibling code in dxf_read_CMC already does both correctly.

2026-08-10  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	in_dxf: MTEXT group 50 is valid — do not reject the whole DXF over it
	Group 50 on MTEXT is the rotation angle, a documented alternative to
	the group 11 direction vector; AutoCAD writes it, and so does ezdxf's
	dimension renderer for every measurement text. The importer knew no
	50 for MTEXT and the unknown-group fallthrough treats any unknown code
	on an importable class as fatal:

	  ERROR: Invalid DXF code 50 for MTEXT
	  ERROR: Failed to decode DXF file

	i.e. one dimension in the drawing and dxf2dwg imported nothing at all.
	Convert the angle to x_axis_dir, as the DXF reference defines it.

2026-08-10  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	dwg_version_as: accept "r12", which every tool's usage lists as valid
	dxf2dwg --help advertises "Valid versions: r12, r14, r2000 (default),
	r2004", but dwg_version_as only knows the table string "r11" for the
	AC1009 format, so --as r12 has never worked. The table is indexed by
	the version enum and R_12 == R_11, so it cannot carry an alias row;
	special-case the alias instead.

	Also print optarg, not argv[1], in the five tools' error message: the
	old message blamed whatever the first flag was, e.g.
	"Invalid version '-y'".

2026-08-10  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	encode: a pre-R13 source must still write handle streams for R13+ targets
	VALUE_HANDLE and FIELD_HANDLE_N chose between two branches with two
	different variables: PRE (R_13b1) keys the r11-index style on the
	TARGET, but the real-handle branch was IF_ENCODE_SINCE_R13, keyed on
	the SOURCE. Encoding an R12 DXF to r2000 satisfied neither: not a
	single handle was written — no owners, no block chains, no control
	entries — and the objects' declared sizes no longer matched their
	content, so readers drowned in "bit_read_RC buffer overflow". Key the
	second branch on the target, complementing the PRE above it.

	in_dxf: R12 layout blocks are named $MODEL_SPACE, not *Model_Space
	An R12 DXF (AutoCAD's own, and every library following the convention,
	e.g. ezdxf) names the layout blocks $MODEL_SPACE/$PAPER_SPACE. Every
	matcher in the importer knows only the r13+ * spellings, so the model
	space block was treated as a user-defined block, BLOCK_CONTROL's
	model_space stayed unset, and every entity of the drawing became
	unreachable after conversion. Normalize the name at the pair reader,
	so all downstream matchers see the canonical spelling.

2026-08-08  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	dxf: do not write R11 JUMP records into the ENTITIES section
	JUMP is not an entity. It is the "resume reading at this offset" marker of
	the R11/R12 entity sections (jump_address, jump_entity_section), and it
	carries no drawing data. DXF has no such record.

	The pre-R13 branch of dxf_entities_write iterates all objects in file order
	and skips the structural markers BLOCK and ENDBLK, but not JUMP. JUMP has no
	ownerhandle, so it lands in the "!ohdl -> assume ms" branch and is emitted as
	"0 JUMP" into ENTITIES. When it falls inside a polyline sequence the file
	stops being loadable: ezdxf 1.4.4 aborts with

	    DXFStructureError: Expected DXF entity JUMP or SEQEND

	decode.c already excludes DWG_TYPE_JUMP when populating the block header
	entity list, for the same reason. The BLOCKS writer is unaffected because it
	requires a matching ownerhandle and drops ownerless objects. Binary DXB is
	unaffected: it walks the owned-entity list, which never contains JUMP.

	Reproduce with the shipped test files:

	    programs/dwg2dxf -o x.dxf test/test-data/r10/entities.dwg
	    grep -c '^JUMP$' x.dxf     # 2 before, 0 after

	Affects programs/entities_r10.dwg and test/test-data/r{9,10}/entities.dwg —
	all three are unreadable by a conformant DXF reader before this change and
	load with 20 entities after it.

	Checked for regressions over all 146 DWGs in test/test-data and programs/,
	converted with stock 0.14.8556 and with this patch, then loaded with ezdxf:
	143 byte-identical, 3 fixed, 0 worse (+60 entities recovered in total).

2026-08-08  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	INSERT: normalize has_attribs for pre-R13 (DXF 66 was written as 128)
	The R11 decoder assigns the masked flag bits straight into has_attribs:

	    DECODER { FIELD_VALUE (has_attribs) = R11FLAG (FLAG_R11_HAS_ATTRIBS); }

	FLAG_R11_HAS_ATTRIBS is 128 and has_attribs is a BITCODE_B documented as
	DXF 66, so every pre-R13 INSERT that has attributes is written out as
	"66 128". DXF group 66 admits only 0 or 1.

	The three sibling POLYLINE sites already normalize with "? 1 : 0"
	(dwg.spec:1345, 1431, 2225); the INSERT one at 749 is the only one that
	does not.

	Reproduce with the shipped test files:

	    programs/dwg2dxf -o x.dxf programs/entities_r10.dwg
	    grep -A1 '^ 66$' x.dxf        # 128 before, 1 after

	Affects programs/entities_r{2.6,2.10,10}.dwg and
	test/test-data/r{2.6,2.10,9,10}/entities.dwg. The dwg2dxf output diff is
	exactly "128" -> "1" on each file; nothing else changes.

