2026-08-07  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	decode_r2007: Reed-Solomon decode uncompressed data pages too
	read_data_section() treats "not compressed" as "not encoded", and copies
	such a page straight out of the file buffer:

	    // only if compressed. TODO: Isn't there a compressed flag as with 2004+?
	    if (section_page->comp_size != section_page->uncomp_size)
	      read_data_page (...)
	    else
	      memcpy (&decomp[section_page->offset], &dat->chain[dat->byte],
	              section_page->uncomp_size);

	But Reed-Solomon coding is independent of compression, and
	read_data_page() already treats the two separately: it undoes the RS
	coding first, then decompresses only `if (size_comp < size_uncomp)`.
	An uncompressed page can therefore still be RS coded, and the memcpy
	hands the caller the RS codewords -- payload interleaved with parity.

	The section page record has no compression flag (the TODO asks about
	exactly that), so page->size is the only evidence: an RS coded page is
	ceil(comp_size/0xFB) * 0xFF rounded up to 32, while a verbatim page is
	only rounded up to 32 and comes out smaller. Take the RS path whenever
	page->size matches the coded size exactly; anything else keeps today's
	verbatim copy.

	In an 11 MB r2007 file here, AcDb:AcDbObjects has 181 pages of which
	136 are stored uncompressed. Every one of them was garbage:

	    pages           objects   fail
	    compressed        10586      0   0.0%
	    uncompressed       2259   2183  96.6%

	The 2183 objects are 2232 LWPOLYLINEs plus a few CIRCLE/MTEXT/TEXT/LINE
	whose handles then appear in *Model_Space's entity list and resolve to
	nothing. Measured entropy of the affected byte ranges is 7.87 bits
	against 6.39 in the intact ones, which is what parity interleaved with
	data looks like.

	Result on that file, against ODA File Converter 25.6:

	    modelspace entities   8588  ->  10847   (ODA: 10847)
	    LWPOLYLINE vertices 231138  -> 1026048  (ODA: 1026048)
	    vertices near DBL_MAX 33188 ->       0  (ODA: 0)

	That also closes #1361, which I filed for the LWPOLYLINE point arrays
	"desynchronising mid-list": they were not desynchronising, they were
	being read out of RS codewords. Same for the 45316 coordinates above
	1e12 reported there -- now 0, where ODA has 40.

	Verification:

	- make check: 270 PASS / 0 FAIL / 0 SKIP, unchanged. Includes the two
	  unit tests covering this area, `decompress_r2007` and
	  `read_data_section: rejects out-of-bounds page->offset`.
	- All 146 DWGs in test/test-data and programs/ converted before and
	  after: 146 byte-identical, 0 worse. None of them stores a data page
	  uncompressed, so none takes the new path.
	- A 190-drawing corpus: three r2007 files go LOAD_FAIL -> OK
	  (+11550, +10847, +5725 entities), 187 unchanged, 0 regressions,
	  0 drawings losing an entity.

	Built on Ubuntu 26.04, gcc 15.2.0, at e405fcff (= tag 0.14.8556).

2026-08-07  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>

	bits: never encode a UTF-16 surrogate into the UTF-8 output
	The UTF-16 to UTF-8 loops encode any code unit >= 0x800 as a three-byte
	sequence, on the strength of this comment:

	    else /* if (c < 0x10000) */
	      {  /* windows ucs-2 has no D800-DC00 surrogate pairs. go straight up */

	U+D800..U+DFFF are surrogate halves. They stand for a code point only in
	pairs, and RFC 3629 forbids encoding them in UTF-8 at all. The decoder in
	this same file already knows that:

	    // reject overlong encodings and UTF-16 surrogates
	    if (cp >= 0x800 && (cp < 0xD800 || cp > 0xDFFF))

	So a string that does contain a surrogate goes out as bytes no UTF-8
	decoder will accept, and a DXF for R2007 or later is UTF-8.

	The file attached to #1021 shows what that costs. One APPID whose name in
	the DWG is damaged:

	    AT_JNT_\xed\xb4\xb8\xe0\xae\xb5\xed\xb4\xb8...

	\xed\xb4\xb8 is U+DD38, a lone low surrogate. iconv -f UTF-8 and Python
	both reject the whole file, and the reporter's AutoCAD refuses the
	drawing with "Error in APPID Table / Invalid or incomplete DXF input --
	drawing canceled". 106000 sound lines lost to one name. ODA File
	Converter reads the same DWG and writes that record as
	$TD_AUDIT_GENERATED_(10E7), so it agrees the name is unusable -- it just
	does not emit anything invalid over it.

	U+FFFD is the standard substitute and occupies the same three bytes, so
	none of these loops changes shape and no buffer arithmetic moves. Four of
	the five drawings in that attachment go from invalid UTF-8 to valid, and
	the DXFs differ by exactly six bytes each -- the two surrogates. The
	fifth had none and is byte-identical.

	Combining a genuine surrogate pair into the supplementary code point it
	denotes would recover more than U+FFFD does, and would emit four bytes
	where six are already reserved. I left it out to keep this to one
	uniform, provable change; happy to follow up with it.

	Verification:

	- make check: 270 PASS / 0 FAIL / 0 SKIP, unchanged. bit_convert_TU has a
	  unit test; its data is BMP-only, so it is unaffected.
	- All 146 DWGs in test/test-data and programs/ converted before and
	  after: 146 byte-identical. None of them carries a surrogate.
	- Three of those 146 do emit invalid UTF-8, and this patch deliberately
	  leaves them alone: example_2000, example_r13 and 2000/TS1 write the
	  degree sign as the single byte 0xB0. They are $ACADVER AC1015 with
	  $DWGCODEPAGE ANSI_1252, and a pre-R2007 DXF is in the drawing's code
	  page, not UTF-8, so 0xB0 is correct there. The rule this patch enforces
	  is only about the UTF-16 conversions.
	- Built and tested on a clean e405fcff (= tag 0.14.8556) with this patch
	  alone. Ubuntu 26.04, gcc 15.2.0.

