	in_dxf: fix stale obj pointer UAF importing VIEWPORT before R2004 (GHSA-qcxp-m6vj-h5h8)
	new_object()'s VIEWPORT -> VX_TABLE_RECORD special case (pre-R2004 DXF,
	group code 5) kept its local Dwg_Object *obj across the dwg_add_VX() call
	used to create the entity's VX_TABLE_RECORD. dwg_add_VX() expands
	API_ADD_TABLE, which can add object(s) and realloc dwg->object[] once the
	object count hits an allocation boundary, invalidating every existing
	pointer into the pool. obj was then read (VIEWPORT tio access,
	SORTENTSTABLE check further down) and written through
	(obj->handle.value = ...; dwg_add_handle()) while stale: a
	heap-use-after-free reachable from untrusted DXF input, with the object
	count controllable via preceding entities and the VIEWPORT handle
	supplied directly by the attacker.

	Save the object's index before the call that may grow the pool, and
	re-fetch obj = &dwg->object[idx] afterwards, matching the pattern already
	used by NEW_OBJECT()/dwg_decode_add_object().

	Add test_viewport_vx_realloc() to test/unit-testing/add_test.c,
	reproducing the advisory's exact boundary (1022 preceding POINT entities
	filling the 1024-slot initial object pool) via dwg_read_dxf() on an
	in-memory buffer, so ASan/valgrind catch any regression. Run before the
	other add_test cases so the object pool's backing allocation is fresh
	enough for the realloc to actually relocate memory.

	Reported-by: rigelyoung (GHSA-qcxp-m6vj-h5h8)

