2026-07-25  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>
	    Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>
	    Claude Fable 5  <noreply@anthropic.com>

	dwg: a duplicate handleref must not disable the ordered-ref index
	ordered_ref_add treats a duplicate (code, absref) pair as "should never
	happen" and reacts by freeing the whole bsearch index, demoting every
	subsequent ordered_ref_find to a linear scan over all refs. But
	duplicates are reachable through a normal path: dwg_add_handleref only
	dedupes via ordered_ref_find on some branches (e.g. obj == NULL), yet
	unconditionally calls ordered_ref_add at the end. One duplicate early in
	a big DXF import turns the whole run quadratic — together with the
	in_dxf strlen fix this took a real 19 MB DXF from >5 minutes (killed) to
	1.8 s, with byte-identical DWG output.

	Refs sharing a key are interchangeable by design (the dedupe branch
	returns whichever is indexed), so simply skip inserting the duplicate
	and keep the index alive.

	Found by IngeCAD's round-trip fidelity harness over a corpus of 1385
	real-world DWGs (same hunt as #1311-#1315).

2026-07-25  Marco Sumari Tellez  <ing.sumari@gmail.com>
	    Claude Fable 5  <noreply@anthropic.com>

	in_dxf: don't strlen() the whole remaining buffer per character
	dxf_read_string's per-character loop probes each byte for a "\M+n" MIF
	escape with memBEGINc, whose implementation calls strlen(s1). Here s
	points into the raw file buffer, so every character of every string
	value scanned the entire remaining file: DXF import cost O(file_size^2).
	A 19 MB DXF (a real pavement-details plan exported by ezdxf) needed
	minutes of pure strlen before this and converts in seconds after.

	Compare the three escape bytes directly with an explicit bounds check
	against dat->size — faster and safer than relying on a terminating NUL
	in a memory-mapped buffer.

	Found by IngeCAD's round-trip fidelity harness over a corpus of 1385
	real-world DWGs (same hunt as #1311-#1315).

