	encode: fix heap-buffer-overflow in preR13 section header encoding
	Two dwg version conversion bugs caused uninitialized section table
	entries, leading to a heap-buffer-overflow in LOG_TRACE when printing
	tbl->name via %s.

	1. dwg_sections_init used realloc to grow the section array but
	   never zeroed the new entries.  Switch to free+calloc so every
	   entry starts zeroed.

	2. encode_check_num_sections extended the array with realloc then
	   memset only zeroed the hole between STYLE and LTYPE (index 4),
	   missing the newly added entries at indices 5+.

	   The memset count was id+1-num_sections when it should cover all
	   new entries: id+2-(num_sections-1).

	* src/dwg.c (dwg_sections_init): Replace realloc with free+calloc.
	* src/encode.c (encode_check_num_sections): Fix memset length.

2026-07-02  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>
	    omp + DeepSeek V4 Pro  <deepseek@deepseek.com>

	encode: fix heap-buffer-overflow in eed/xdata TU conversion
	The fuzzer found a heap-buffer-overflow at src/encode.c:6861 in
	dwg_encode_eed_data and at line 7612 in dwg_encode_xdata.

	bit_utf8_to_TU allocates its result buffer with calloc(strlen(str)+1, 2),
	but the callers iterated dest[j] up to a separately-stored length field
	(data->u.eed_0.length / rbuf->value.str.size).  When a fuzzer-supplied
	input contains an embedded NUL byte, strlen() returns less than that
	stored length, the TU buffer is undersized, and the loop reads past the
	heap allocation.

	Fix by clamping the loop bound to the actual strlen at both sites.
	For valid data the clamp is a no-op; for corrupted inputs it prevents
	the OOB access.

	* src/encode.c (dwg_encode_eed_data): Guard length against strlen.
	  (dwg_encode_xdata): Likewise.

