2026-07-01  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	decode: fix heap-buffer-overflow WRITE in decompress_R2004_section (GHSA-qg2f-8389-w95j)
	`read_2004_compressed_section` allocates `dec.chain` as
	`calloc(max_decomp_size, 1)` with `dec.size = max_decomp_size`.
	Inside the per-section loop it overwrote `dec.size` with the smaller
	value `dec.byte + info->max_decomp_size`.  `bit_chain_alloc_size`
	assumes `dat->size == capacity(dat->chain)` and reallocs to
	`dat->size + CHAIN_BLOCK`; because `dec.size` was reset to a much
	smaller number the realloc physically shrinks the live buffer while
	`dec.byte` and `dec.size` keep tracking the larger logical stream.
	The existing bounds guard in `decompress_R2004_section`
	(`end > dec->size`) passes because `dec->size` itself is the shrunken
	(but still larger than the real allocation) value, so the copy lands
	past the shrunk allocation — a heap-buffer-overflow WRITE of up to
	several kilobytes.

	The CVE-2026-9501 guard (commit e501cb99) was structurally unable to
	detect this: it validated against the same `dec->size` that drove the
	shrink.

	Fix: remove the per-section `dec.size` assignment.  `dec.size` stays
	pinned to `max_decomp_size` (the true allocation) for the entire
	decompression lifetime.  The pre-loop guard already asserts
	`es.fields.address + info->max_decomp_size <= max_decomp_size`, so
	the bounds check in `decompress_R2004_section` (`end > dec->size`)
	remains correct and sufficient.  `bit_chain_alloc` can now only grow
	the buffer, never shrink it.

	Fixes GHSA-qg2f-8389-w95j. Related: #1180 #1239 #1243 #1251 #1254.
	Vuln: CWE-122
	CVE requested.
	Severity: 6.7/10 Moderate
	CVSS:4.0/AV:L/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:A/VC:N/VI:N/VA:H/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N

	* src/decode.c (read_2004_compressed_section): remove dec.size override
	  inside the per-section loop.

	Reported-by: yPin9 <ypp0x7@gmail.com> Taipeh
	Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-07-01  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	bits: fix UBSan negation overflow in bit_read_MC
	BITCODE_UMC is uint64_t, so the 5-byte modular-char accumulator can
	hold 35 bits.  The old guard `result == 0x80000000` only caught the
	exact case where the lower 32 bits were 0x80000000 with no upper bits
	set.  Values such as 0x180000000 pass the guard, truncate to INT32_MIN
	on the `(BITCODE_MC)result` cast, and then negating INT32_MIN is
	undefined behaviour (UBSan: "negation of -2147483648 cannot be
	represented in type 'int'").

	Fix: replace the point check with `result > INT32_MAX`.  Any magnitude
	exceeding INT32_MAX cannot be safely round-tripped through int32_t
	negation, so go to the existing err_mc error path instead.
	Fixes ossfuzz 444908414 (and GH #1153 again)

	* src/bits.c (bit_read_MC): use result > INT32_MAX guard.

	Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-07-01  Reini Urban  <reini.urban@gmail.com>

	in_dxf: fix double-free of dxfname in dxf_blocks_read
	After calling new_object(), the caller's dxfname may already have been
	freed by UPGRADE_ENTITY inside new_object (which frees obj->dxfname,
	which aliases the caller's dxfname, and sets only the local variable to
	NULL). Without NULLing the caller's dxfname, the subsequent
	`free (dxfname)` in the error path double-frees the pointer.

	The other two new_object call sites in dxf_entities_read (line ~13934)
	and dxf_blocks_read (line ~14026) already had this guard; the blocks
	loop at ~13605 was missing it.

	Reported by oss-fuzz (testcase 5692626108743680, heap-double-free in
	dxf_blocks_read).

