2026-06-15  Pascal Bourguignon  <pjb@informatimago.com>

	decode: fix R2004+ decompression aborting on exact buffer-fill (data loss)
	decompress_R2004_section rejected a back-reference copy whose end landed
	*exactly* on the decompressed buffer size (`end >= dec->size`). A real R2018
	section legitimately ends with a copy that fills the buffer to the brim (the
	trailing run lands in the unused tail past the page's real data and is never
	read), so this false positive aborted the whole section mid-stream with
	"Invalid decompression bytes". The short section then left every later
	bit_read run off the end, dropping/garbling thousands of objects — a
	regression vs 0.13.3 introduced by the 2004-decompression refactor (the
	pre-refactor code used `dst + comp_bytes > dst_end`, i.e. strict `>`).

	Restore the strict comparison (`end > dec->size`): only error when the copy
	would actually overrun the buffer; allow the exact-fill case. The copy then
	writes at most dec->size bytes (buffer is over-allocated by 1024), so this is
	still safe against OOB.

	On a real R2018 file that previously hit 5+ "Invalid decompression bytes"
	and produced unusable JSON, the decode is now clean (0 errors) and recovers
	the same 121 objects as 0.13.3. r2004–r2018 corpus decode unaffected.

	Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-06-15  Pascal Bourguignon  <pjb@informatimago.com>

	encode: fix R2018 decode→encode→decode object loss and thumbnail growth
	Two defects broke faithful same-version round-trips of R2004+ DWGs
	(R2018/AC1032 in particular); both are fixed in encode_r13_thumbnail and
	the dwg_encode_add_object dispatch.

	1. UNKNOWN_OBJ/UNKNOWN_ENT proxy objects (custom classes libredwg keeps as
	   raw blobs) were only re-emitted when the input was JSON
	   (DWG_OPTS_INJSON). A DWG→DWG re-encode at the same version wrote just the
	   common header and dropped the blob, producing "Wrong object size" errors
	   and losing objects (6 dropped, 474→473 on example_2018). Gate on
	   version == from_version && unknown_bits instead, so DWG and JSON inputs
	   are handled identically (the raw encoder already requires same-version).

	2. The AcDb:Preview thumbnail grew 16 bytes on every round-trip and tripped
	   "Preview overflow". The r2004+ decoder keeps the trailing THUMBNAIL_END
	   sentinel inside thumbnail.size (decode.c: size = sec_dat.size - 16,
	   byte = 16; the JSON writer likewise retains it), so encode_r13_thumbnail
	   must not append its own END sentinel for SINCE(R_2004a) — it is already
	   part of the content. r13-r2000 keep the END separate and still write it.
	   Also write content from chain + thumbnail.byte so the leading sentinel is
	   skipped.

	Result: dwgrewrite --as r2018 on example_2018/sample_2018 yields 474/474
	objects, a byte-identical thumbnail stable across repeated round-trips, and
	no new errors; r2010/r2013 and the r13/r14/r2000 paths are unaffected.

	Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-06-15  Pascal Bourguignon  <pjb@informatimago.com>

	in_dxf: preserve leading spaces in string values; fix r2007+ DWGCODEPAGE read
	Two DXF-import string fidelity fixes:

	- dxf_read_string stripped leading whitespace from every value via
	  dxf_skip_ws, but dxf_read_rs already consumed the group-code line and its
	  newline, so we are at the value-line start where leading spaces are
	  significant. An MTEXT continuation chunk beginning with a space (e.g.
	  "...un des" + " maîtres...") lost that space, joining the words. Drop the
	  skip.

	- dxf_set_DWGCODEPAGE read header_vars.DWGCODEPAGE as a C string, but r2007+
	  stores header TV vars as TU (UTF-16), so "ANSI_1252" was seen as "A" and
	  the codepage came out UNDEFINED ("Invalid DWGCODEPAGE A"). Decode the TU
	  name first, detected by the embedded NUL (codepage names are pure ASCII).

	With these and the bit_write_T UTF-8 fix, a real R2018 multiline-MTEXT
	dwg2dxf | dxf2dwg --as r2018 round-trip reproduces the text byte-identically.

	Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>

2026-06-15  Pascal Bourguignon  <pjb@informatimago.com>

	bits: decode UTF-8 DXF strings to UTF-16 when encoding to r2007+
	bit_write_T, when emitting a non-TU (DXF/JSON/API) string to an r2007+
	target, built the UTF-16 by copying each input byte and zero-extending it.
	For DXF input — whose in-memory strings are UTF-8 (in_dxf keeps them as
	read) — that double-encoded every non-ASCII character: "été" was written as
	"Ã©tÃ©". A decode→DXF→r2018 round-trip of a French multiline MTEXT thus
	mojibaked all accented text (non-ASCII char count doubled).

	Decode real UTF-8 multi-byte sequences (2/3-byte, and 4-byte to a surrogate
